WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.600
UN member states will consider a new&nbsp;
Russian-led treaty on cybercrime.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:16.480
Having hacked US infrastructure, interfered&nbsp;
in US elections, and cracked down on its own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:22.160
cyberspace with authoritarian zeal, the Russian&nbsp;
government knows the subject well. Nonetheless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:27.840
despite its abysmal record, Moscow’s efforts&nbsp;
to rewrite global cybercrime regulations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:31.520
have already been greeted with&nbsp;
some initial success at the UN.

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:36.160
These developments are part of a larger&nbsp;
trend of Russia coopting UN cyber norms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:38.480
together with China. In January,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:43.040
the two nations attempted to hijack the&nbsp;
UN’s International Telecommunication Union,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:48.000
which determines the rules of internet governance,&nbsp;
to make the web more autocrat-friendly.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.200
Moscow and Beijing have already turned the&nbsp;
internet into a tool for domestic repression.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:57.120
Allowing them to dictate the rules&nbsp;
for the rest of the world is absurd.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:02.480
The US must rally its allies to stop autocrats’&nbsp;
push to rewrite the rules of cyberspace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:05.680
and curb Russia and China’s&nbsp;
influence in the UN more broadly.

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:10.720
Who do you think should govern the&nbsp;
internet? Let us know in our poll.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:15.360
Also, let us know what other topics you'd&nbsp;
like our scholars to cover in 60 seconds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:19.280
and be sure to like and subscribe for&nbsp;
more research and videos from AEI.

